messages.content:
Cutting the number of DOM nodes is what matters. Both options do that, so pick by usability.

In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks, 30 page loads of a table with 10,000 rows built from 14 elements per row used ~1036 J. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page, as a plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`, used ~457 J. That is about 56% less. That benchmark measured pagination, not virtual scrolling, so I can't give you a figure for virtualization. It removes the same nodes, so expect the same direction.

- **Pagination:** simplest. Page size is fixed, Ctrl+F only searches the current page, and sort and filter must run on the data array, not on the DOM.
- **Virtual scrolling:** better if people scroll through the whole sweep. It needs fixed row height and more code.
- **Either way:** drop the per-cell wrapper spans. They multiply the node count for no gain.

```javascript
const PAGE_SIZE = 100;

function renderPage(tbody, rows, page) {
  const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
  for (const r of rows.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
    const tr = document.createElement('tr');
    for (const v of r) tr.insertCell().textContent = v;
    frag.appendChild(tr);
  }
  tbody.replaceChildren(frag);
}
```

For a table with a few hundred rows, none of this is worth the code.

hi i made a small node cli tool "diskscan" that scans a folder and opens a local page in the browser to show progress. the page has a spinner so you see its working. my laptop fan gets loud when the scan runs for long and i dont know whats wrong. maybe the spinner? this is the code of the spinner in public/app.js

```js
const spinner = document.getElementById('spinner');
const label = document.getElementById('label');
let angle = 0;

const timer = setInterval(() => {
  angle = (angle + 6) % 360;
  spinner.style.transform = 'rotate(' + angle + 'deg)';
}, 16);

const events = new EventSource('/progress');
events.onmessage = (e) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
  label.textContent = data.files + ' files, ' + data.mb + ' MB';
  if (data.done) {
    clearInterval(timer);
    events.close();
    label.textContent += ' - finished';
  }
};
```

how much energy would i save if i do the spinner in another way?

Some, but the spinner is probably not the main reason for the fan. The scan itself, running in your Node process, is the more likely cause.

Your timer runs about 60 times per second. Each tick runs JavaScript that writes a new `transform` style. A CSS animation lets the browser do the same rotation without your script running every frame.

In Green Metrics Tool browser benchmarks, a 20-second animation used ~161 J of CPU energy when JavaScript updated styles every frame with `requestAnimationFrame`. The same animation with CSS `@keyframes` used ~145 J, about 10% less. Your timer uses `setInterval`, not `requestAnimationFrame`, but it does the same kind of work: script wakes up and writes a style every frame.

```css
#spinner {
  animation: spin 1s linear infinite;
}

#spinner.done {
  animation: none;
}

@keyframes spin {
  to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
```

```js
const spinner = document.getElementById('spinner');
const label = document.getElementById('label');

const events = new EventSource('/progress');
events.onmessage = (e) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
  label.textContent = data.files + ' files, ' + data.mb + ' MB';
  if (data.done) {
    spinner.classList.add('done');
    events.close();
    label.textContent += ' - finished';
  }
};
```

- The animation only changes `transform`, so the browser can run it without recalculating the page layout.
- The spinner stops when the scan finishes, as before.
- The 10% is for the animation alone, measured in one benchmark. It will not apply to your whole laptop or to your whole scan.

To find out what causes the fan, compare the browser task manager with `top` for the Node process while a scan runs. To measure the spinner change, run both versions for the same time in a Green Metrics Tool usage scenario, or at least compare CPU time with `perf stat`.

