```javascript
// dq-report.js: renders rejected rows from the nightly warehouse load
// reviewer on PR #412: "this should be paginated, rendering everything wastes energy"
// is it actually worth it? bad nights have 10-20k rejects, ~40 people open this every morning

async function loadRejects(runId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/etl/runs/${runId}/rejects`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`rejects request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

function renderRejects(rows) {
  const container = document.getElementById('rejects');
  container.innerHTML = '';
  document.getElementById('reject-count').textContent = rows.length;

  for (const r of rows) {
    const row = document.createElement('div');
    row.className = 'reject-row';
    for (const [key, value] of [
      ['source', r.source_file],
      ['line', r.line_no],
      ['rule', r.rule],
      ['field', r.field],
      ['value', r.raw_value],
      ['message', r.message],
    ]) {
      const cell = document.createElement('div');
      cell.className = `cell cell-${key}`;
      const inner = document.createElement('span');
      inner.textContent = value;
      cell.appendChild(inner);
      row.appendChild(cell);
    }
    container.appendChild(row);
  }
}

document.getElementById('run-select').addEventListener('change', async (e) => {
  const rows = await loadRejects(e.target.value);
  renderRejects(rows);
});
```

?

Short answer: yes, your reviewer is right, and the saving repeats on every open.

**What drives the cost:** each reject becomes 13 elements (a row `div`, 6 cell `div`s, 6 `span`s). Every row is appended to the live DOM on its own. On a bad night that is over a hundred thousand elements. The browser must create, style and lay out all of them on every open, and it keeps them in memory while the tab stays open. That cost grows with the reject count. A paginated page stays at one page of rows, no matter how bad the night was.

**Measured reference:** in Green Metrics Tool page-load benchmarks, 30 loads of a table with 10,000 rows built from 14 elements per row, appended to the live DOM one by one, used ~1036 J and 72 s. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page, as a plain `<table>` built in a `DocumentFragment`, used ~457 J and 36 s. That is about 56% less (about 2.3× less). That setup is close to yours. It is still a different page and machine, so I won't turn it into a figure for your 40 opens a day.

The change that was measured, applied to your report:

```html
<table id="rejects">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Source</th><th>Line</th><th>Rule</th><th>Field</th><th>Value</th><th>Message</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody></tbody>
</table>
<button id="prev">Prev</button> <span id="page-info"></span> <button id="next">Next</button>
```

```javascript
const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
const COLUMNS = ['source_file', 'line_no', 'rule', 'field', 'raw_value', 'message'];
let rejects = [];
let page = 0;

async function loadRejects(runId) {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/etl/runs/${runId}/rejects`);
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`rejects request failed: ${res.status}`);
  return res.json();
}

function renderPage() {
  const start = page * PAGE_SIZE;
  const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
  for (const r of rejects.slice(start, start + PAGE_SIZE)) {
    const tr = document.createElement('tr');
    for (const key of COLUMNS) {
      const td = document.createElement('td');
      td.textContent = r[key];
      tr.appendChild(td);
    }
    frag.appendChild(tr);
  }
  document.querySelector('#rejects tbody').replaceChildren(frag);

  const pages = Math.max(1, Math.ceil(rejects.length / PAGE_SIZE));
  document.getElementById('page-info').textContent = `Page ${page + 1} of ${pages}`;
  document.getElementById('prev').disabled = page === 0;
  document.getElementById('next').disabled = page >= pages - 1;
}

document.getElementById('run-select').addEventListener('change', async (e) => {
  rejects = await loadRejects(e.target.value);
  page = 0;
  document.getElementById('reject-count').textContent = rejects.length;
  renderPage();
});
document.getElementById('prev').addEventListener('click', () => { page--; renderPage(); });
document.getElementById('next').addEventListener('click', () => { page++; renderPage(); });
```

Things that change for users:
- The browser's Ctrl+F only finds rejects on the current page. If people search for a file or rule, add a filter input that filters `rejects` before paging.
- Styles on `.reject-row` / `.cell-*` need to move to the table (`#rejects td:nth-child(5)` or classes on the `td`s).

**How to measure it for your page:** load the report for a large run once with each version.
- For energy, run both as Green Metrics Tool usage scenarios that open the page in a browser.
- As a quick proxy, compare scripting and rendering time in the browser's DevTools Performance panel.
- Check `document.getElementsByTagName('*').length` to see the element count drop.

