Your config sends all files uncompressed. For HTML, CSS, JS and SVG that wastes a lot of bytes, and on mobile networks it is the bytes that make the page slow.

```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name launch.example.com;
    root /var/www/launch;
    index index.html;

    access_log /var/log/nginx/launch.access.log;
    error_log  /var/log/nginx/launch.error.log;

    # Serve app.js.gz if it exists next to app.js
    gzip_static on;

    # Fallback: compress text files that have no .gz version
    gzip on;
    gzip_comp_level 5;
    gzip_vary on;
    gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    location ~* \.(css|js|png|jpg|jpeg|svg|woff2)$ {
        expires 30d;
        access_log off;
    }
}
```

**Green Notes**
- **Enable text compression** (reduces network): in Green Metrics Tool benchmarks with nginx serving 2,000 requests for text assets, compression sent about 10× fewer bytes. This is what helps your mobile visitors.
- **Precompressed files** (reduces E): with `gzip_static`, the files are compressed once at deploy instead of on every request. In the same benchmark, gzip on every request cost about 4× the CPU energy of serving precompressed files.

To use this, create the `.gz` files when you deploy, e.g. `gzip -k -9` on the HTML, CSS, JS and SVG files. Recreate them on every deploy. Otherwise nginx serves an old `app.js.gz` after `app.js` has changed. `text/html` is compressed by default, so it is not in `gzip_types`.

Yes. Remove `gzip_static` and keep the rest:

```nginx
gzip on;
gzip_comp_level 5;
gzip_vary on;
gzip_types text/css application/javascript application/json image/svg+xml;
```

Now nginx compresses every text response itself, and stale `.gz` files cannot happen. This costs some CPU per request. For a launch page the byte saving on mobile matters more, and level 5 keeps that CPU cost moderate.

If traffic gets big later, a small cron job that creates the `.gz` files after uploads would let you turn `gzip_static` back on.

