**Workload:** web (internal log viewer, loaded in the browser by about 15 people a day)

#### [Bug] The mono font is never used
- **Where:** `.log-line { font-family: 'JetBrains Mono', ... }`
- **Issue:** the `@font-face` rules declare the family as `JetBrainsMono` (no space), but `.log-line` asks for `JetBrains Mono` (with space). The names don't match, so the browser downloads the font files and then ignores them. The log lines fall back to the default `monospace`. That is why the fonts "look wrong sometimes".
- **Fix:** the fix below removes the problem, because no custom mono font is loaded. If you keep the font files, use the same name in both places.

#### [Medium] Six TTF files to read text
- **Where:** the six `@font-face` blocks
- **Issue:** every cold page load fetches up to six full TTF files, and the browser has to parse them. For a log viewer this is the main cost of the stylesheet. In Green Metrics Tool micro-benchmarks of 30 cold page loads, six local TTF files used ~556 J, two Latin-subset WOFF2 files ~488 J (about 12% less), and the system font stack ~410 J (about 26% less than TTF).
- **Fix:** use the system font stacks. A log viewer does not need a brand font, and system monospace fonts are good for logs:
  ```css
  body {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: system-ui, -apple-system, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, sans-serif;
    background: #101418;
