**Workload:** web (ML evaluation report)

#### [Bug] Loop reads one element past the end
- **Where:** `for (let i = 0; i <= preds.length; i++)`
- **Issue:** on the last pass `preds[i]` is `undefined`, so `p.pred` throws a `TypeError`. The rows are already drawn by then, so you don't see it on the page. It only shows up as an error in the console.
- **Fix:** use `i < preds.length`, or `for (const p of preds)`.

#### [Security] File names and labels inserted with `innerHTML`
- **Where:** `row.innerHTML = ...`
- **Issue:** `p.file` and `p.label` are parsed as HTML. A file name with `<`, `&` or `"` breaks the row. If the dataset ever contains names you don't control (scraped or uploaded images), it becomes an HTML injection.
- **Fix:** build the elements and set `textContent`, and set the image `src` as a property. The full page at the end does this.

#### [High] 12,400 rows rendered at once, with wrappers around every cell
- **Where:** the `for` loop that fills `#preds`
- **Issue:** this is the freeze. Every row has about a dozen elements: a row `div`, five cell `div`s, a `thumb` `div` and four `span`s. With 12,400 rows that is close to 150,000 elements. Each row is also appended to the live page one by one. On top of that, all 12,400 thumbnails start downloading and decoding right away.
- **Fix:** show 100 rows per page in a plain `<table>`. Build each page in a `DocumentFragment` and insert it in one step. Drop the extra `div`/`span` wrappers.
- **Impact:** E on the client, and far fewer image requests per open. In Green Metrics Tool measurements, 30 page loads of a 10,000-row table with wrappers around every cell, appended to the live DOM one by one, used ~1036 J of CPU energy. The same data paginated to 100 rows per page as a plain table built in a `DocumentFragment` used ~457 J, about 56% less.

#### [Medium] `loading="lazy"` is on the wrong image
- **Where:** the confusion matrix `<img>` and the thumbnail `<img>`
- **Issue:** the confusion matrix is the first thing on the page, so lazy loading only delays it. The thumbnails are the images below the fold, and they have no `loading` attribute and no size. Without a size, lazy images have zero height before they load, so many of them would count as visible and load anyway.
- **Fix:** remove `loading="lazy"` from the confusion matrix. Add it to the thumbnails, together with `width` and `height`.
- **Impact:** network and E, but only for rows nobody scrolls to. In Green Metrics Tool measurements of an article with 15 photos, loads without scrolling used about 12% less CPU energy with lazy images below the fold. When visitors scrolled to the end, there was no difference. If you check every row of a page, pagination is what helps you. Lazy loading helps when you only look at the top rows.

Fixed page:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>Eval run – birdnet-v3 on test split</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="report.css">
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <h1>birdnet-v3 · test split (12,400 images)</h1>
    <img src="plots/confusion_matrix.png" alt="Confusion matrix" width="900" height="900">
    <p id="summary"></p>
  </header>

  <table id="preds">
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>File</th><th>Label</th><th>Prediction</th><th>Confidence</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody></tbody>
  </table>
  <nav>
    <button id="prev">Previous</button>
    <span id="page"></span>
    <button id="next">Next</button>
  </nav>

  <script>
    const PAGE_SIZE = 100;
    let preds = [];
    let page = 0;

    function addCell(tr, text) {
      tr.insertCell().textContent = text;
    }

    function render() {
      const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
      for (const p of preds.slice(page * PAGE_SIZE, (page + 1) * PAGE_SIZE)) {
        const tr = document.createElement("tr");
        if (p.pred !== p.label) tr.className = "wrong";

        const img = document.createElement("img");
        img.loading = "lazy";          // set before src
        img.width = 64;
        img.height = 64;
        img.alt = "";
        img.src = "thumbs/" + p.file;
        tr.insertCell().append(img);

        addCell(tr, p.file);
        addCell(tr, p.label);
        addCell(tr, p.pred);
        addCell(tr, (p.conf * 100).toFixed(1) + "%");
        frag.append(tr);
      }
      document.querySelector("#preds tbody").replaceChildren(frag);

      const pages = Math.ceil(preds.length / PAGE_SIZE);
      document.getElementById("page").textContent = `Page ${page + 1} of ${pages}`;
      document.getElementById("prev").disabled = page === 0;
      document.getElementById("next").disabled = page >= pages - 1;
    }

    document.getElementById("prev").onclick = () => { page--; render(); };
    document.getElementById("next").onclick = () => { page++; render(); };

    fetch("predictions.json")
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(data => {
        preds = data;
        const wrong = preds.filter(p => p.pred !== p.label).length;
        document.getElementById("summary").textContent =
          `Accuracy: ${((1 - wrong / preds.length) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`;
        render();
      });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

